\section{Product Decisions of Young Firms}

Are there differences in the product and patenting decisions of high-growth young firms that separates them from other young firms? I take the subsample of all firm-year observations that are classified as young (5 year threshold) when covered by the EAP. I create variable $D^{HG}_{ikt}$ equal to 1 if the firm is in the top 30\% of employment or revenue growth across the subsample of young firms in a given year and 0 otherwise. 

Table \ref{tab: summary_stats_top_growth_empl_growth} presents summary statistics of high-growth young firms, defined by employment and revenue growth, respectively. High growth young firms have fewer employees and revenue than non-high-growth young firms. On average they have higher patenting and trademarking rates.

To capture the relation between firm growth and the product decisions of high-growth young firms, we run the following regression:
    

\[
Y_{ikt} =  \beta^N D^N_{ik\hat{t}} + \beta^X D^X_{ik\hat{t}} + \sum_{I \in \{P, TM\}} \left( \beta^I D^I_{ik\hat{t}} + \beta^{I \times N} \left( D^I_{ik\hat{t}} \times D^N_{ik\hat{t}} \right) \right) + X_{ik\hat{t}} + \alpha_k + \alpha_t + \varepsilon_{ikt}.
\]


Where $Y_{ikt}$ can be either $D^{HG}_{ikt}$ or employment or revenue growth rate. $D_{ikt}$ are dummy indicators for firm $i$ in industry $k$ and time $\hat{t}\in [t-2,t]$. $N$ is net product creation, meaning whether the difference in the number of products introduced and destroyed by a firm in a year is positive. $X$ is product destruction, meaning whether the difference  in the number of products introduced and destroyed by a firm in a year is negative. $P$ is whether the firm patented. $TM$ is whether the firm introduced a trademark. $\alpha_{k}$ and $\alpha_{t}$ are industry and time fixed effects, respectively.  I run each regression separately for the sample of small, medium and large firms. All regressions are weighted by average employment or revenue, depending on the dependent variable.


